      housing: "A Beagle adapts to an apartment or a house on square footage alone, but containment is the real constraint. The AKC describes them as escape artists and specifies a fence at least five feet tall that extends underground to stop tunneling, and that is the single largest setup cost of the breed. Their short dense double coat gets heavier in winter and they handle cool weather better than heat, though they are not an outdoor-living breed: a Beagle left alone for long stretches, inside or out, tends to become destructive, because they were bred to work in packs and are happiest with company. Plan for the voice as well as the fence, since the baying that made them useful in the field carries a long way in a suburban yard. If you rent or share a fence line, resolve containment and noise before the puppy arrives rather than after.",
      diet: "Weight control is the central feeding task for this breed, not an afterthought. Obesity was the most commonly diagnosed disorder in UK Beagles at 24.27 percent, and Beagles showed roughly 2.67 times the odds of overweight status in a UK study of 22,333 dogs. Measure every meal with a cup or a scale rather than free-feeding, and count training treats against the daily ration. Excess weight worsens arthritis twice over, directly through joint loading and indirectly because fat cells release pro-inflammatory mediators. Body condition scoring every month catches drift earlier than the bathroom scale does. Secure your trash and your counters too, because a scent-driven dog that finds food once will keep checking for the rest of its life.",
