      enrichment: "This is a scent hound with a working brain, and the frustration of an under-stimulated Dachshund shows up as digging, barking, and destructive behavior long before it shows up as boredom. Scent work suits them exactly: snuffle mats, hide-and-find games, and formal nose work all satisfy the original job without asking the spine to do anything athletic. Keep the exercise low-impact and level. Steady walks are excellent. Repeated jumping, stair sprints, and hard twisting play are not.",
      health: "Intervertebral disc disease (IVDD) is the defining health issue of the breed, and it is genetic rather than incidental. Intervertebral discs are the shock-absorbing cushions between the vertebrae, and in chondrodystrophic breeds they calcify and lose their cushioning early, sometimes in the first two or three years of life. A calcified disc can then extrude into the spinal canal, and the resulting compression ranges from pain to complete hind-limb paralysis. Learn the early signs, which are reluctance to jump, a hunched or tense back, yelping when picked up, and a wobbly or dragging back end, and treat any of them as an emergency vet visit rather than a wait-and-see. Beyond the spine, the breed sees progressive retinal atrophy, patellar luxation, dental crowding from a small jaw, and obesity. Ask a breeder about spinal radiographic screening for disc calcification in the parents.",
